WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:02.980
Did he, or didn't he?

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:06.581
Joker is a film that challenges its audience
to distinguish between fantasy and reality,

00:00:06.581 --> 00:00:11.610
and the task is never more harrowing than
in the final interaction between Arthur Fleck

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:13.650
and his would-be girlfriend Sophie.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:18.230
It's particularly maddening because it's impossible
to know for sure what went down - unless you

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:20.130
have a voice from on high to clue you in.

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:24.320
Let's get to the bottom of things, and be
advised: spoilers ahead.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.359
The film's director Todd Phillips recently
went on record as to whether or not Arthur

00:00:28.359 --> 00:00:33.380
actually killed Sophie near the film's end,
and we'll get to his comments in just a moment.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:36.420
First, though, let's do a little refresher
course on what happens.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:41.110
In the movie, failed comedian Arthur slowly
transforms into the amoral Clown Prince of

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:45.710
Crime after a series of humiliations, rejections,
and outright physical beatings.

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:50.900
It seems that the only thing that may be helping
him cling to sanity is his burgeoning relationship

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:55.300
with his kind, pretty neighbor Sophie, one
of the only people in his life who seems to

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:58.920
accept him for the extremely odd, troubled
man he is.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.230
"It's so awful, isn't it."

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:07.030
The final straw comes when Arthur discovers
a letter from his extremely ill, unstable

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:11.890
mother Penny which indicates that he is the
illegitimate son of billionaire Thomas Wayne,

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:14.880
who is in the thick of Gotham City's mayoral
race.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.030
After a confrontation with Wayne, who tells
Arthur in no uncertain terms that his mother

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:23.980
is delusional, Arthur pays a visit to Penny
- a visit that turns shockingly deadly, as

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:26.679
Arthur kills his mother and flees the scene.

00:01:26.679 --> 00:01:31.640
His next stop is Sophie's apartment, and when
he arrives, it becomes clear that the relationship

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:32.999
has all been in Arthur's head.

00:01:32.999 --> 00:01:37.350
Sophie recognizes him as nothing more than
her neighbor, and she insists that he leave.

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:42.270
He does - but given his state of mind, his
extremely recent matricidal activities, and

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:46.670
the fact that Sophie is never seen again,
fans have speculated that Arthur must have

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:48.109
done the poor girl in.

00:01:48.109 --> 00:01:52.220
Joker's cinematographer Lawrence Sher has
weighed in on whether or not Arthur killed

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:57.539
Sophie in the past, and now, Phillips has
confirmed Sher's take on the situation.

00:01:57.539 --> 00:02:01.600
Not only is there a definitive answer, but
it turns out that a scene was cut from the

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.780
film which would have confirmed Sophie's fate.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:07.240
The short answer is no, Arthur did not kill
Sophie.

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:11.039
The simple reason: even in his twisted mind,
it wouldn't have made sense.

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:15.860
Sher alluded to this when he made his remarks
to Slashfilm in late October 2019.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:16.860
As he explained,

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:19.069
"Some people have asked me, 'Was she killed?'

00:02:19.069 --> 00:02:21.439
[Phillips] makes it clear she wasn't killed.

00:02:21.439 --> 00:02:25.530
Arthur is killing people who've wronged him
in a certain way, and Sophie never wronged

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:26.530
him."

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:30.340
Phillips expanded on this during a more recent
conversation with IndieWire, saying, quote,

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:32.760
"He doesn't kill her, definitively."

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.950
"As the filmmaker and the writer I am saying
he doesn't kill her.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:40.920
We like the idea that it's almost like a litmus
test for the audience to say, 'How crazy is

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:41.920
he?'"

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:45.700
Phillips went on to say that most people he's
spoken to about the movie seem to understand

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:50.580
that Sophie survived, and that Arthur's character
wouldn't have taken that extreme step of doing

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:51.659
her harm.

00:02:51.659 --> 00:02:53.980
"He clearly has a code he lives by.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:57.739
Of course he did not kill this woman down
the hall and her child."

00:02:57.739 --> 00:03:02.540
Phillips went on to describe a short deleted
scene which was intercut with Arthur's appearance

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:04.110
on Murray Franklin's talk show.

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:09.020
In it, Sophie was seen at home, watching the
proceedings on television with growing horror;

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:13.379
while the scene would have unambiguously confirmed
that Arthur did indeed leave Sophie alive

00:03:13.379 --> 00:03:16.420
and well, it was cut for a pretty darn good
reason.

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:17.450
According to Phillips,

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:22.340
"We made a choice in the film to only show
everything from Joaquin's perspective...We

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:26.250
cut out a great scene with Marc Maron and
Bob De Niro that didn't involve Joaquin, because

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:27.930
it didn't involve Joaquin.

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:31.750
Everything was being told through his point
of view and perspective, so to suddenly cut

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:35.540
to this woman, it would just literally [have
changed] the DNA of the film."

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:37.400
Well, that certainly makes perfect sense.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:41.390
We can't help but think, however, that the
side effect of leaving Sophie's fate up in

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:44.310
the air may have cemented that scene's fate.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:48.359
Joker is a film that is absolutely meant to
be open to multiple interpretations, and the

00:03:48.359 --> 00:03:52.760
question of whether Arthur killed his fantasy
love interest is one of the film's great mysteries

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:54.189
upon first viewing.

00:03:54.189 --> 00:03:56.239
That mystery, though, has now been solved.

00:03:56.239 --> 00:04:00.599
We wouldn't expect Phillips to shed much light
on any more of Joker's scintillating questions,

00:04:00.599 --> 00:04:05.430
however - up to and including whether or not
any of the movie's events actually happened,

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:09.890
or whether they all took place in Arthur's
troubled mind, as many fans have speculated.

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:14.510
Sure, we now know that Sophie is safe at the
film's end...unless, of course, she never

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:15.810
existed at all.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:20.389
With a movie like Joker, answers have a way
of just leading to more questions.

